WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.569
Some IPC designs transfer more than data.

2
00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:09.540
They pass operating-system handles, file descriptors, shared-memory objects, memory mappings, temporary file references, named resources, or other

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.641
capability-bearing values.

4
00:00:10.641 --> 00:00:19.081
Those transfers are review-sensitive because the receiver may gain access to a file, device, process object, memory region, event, or resource that

5
00:00:19.081 --> 00:00:19.814
carries authority.

6
00:00:19.814 --> 00:00:22.016
A handle is not harmless metadata.

7
00:00:22.016 --> 00:00:31.923
The contract should define what the handle refers to, which permissions it carries, who owns it, who may duplicate it, who may close it, and how long

8
00:00:31.923 --> 00:00:33.024
it remains valid.

9
00:00:33.024 --> 00:00:39.262
The receiver should not assume that a transferred resource is safe merely because the sender is local.

10
00:00:39.262 --> 00:00:42.931
Access rights, expected type, size, and lifetime still need validation.

11
00:00:42.931 --> 00:00:45.133
Shared memory needs especially clear rules.

12
00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:51.371
Both sides need agreement on mapping size, layout, synchronization, read and write permissions, initialization state, and cleanup.

13
00:00:51.371 --> 00:00:55.774
A size stored in shared memory should not be trusted without bounds.

14
00:00:55.774 --> 00:01:03.479
A writer should not be able to change data while a reader assumes it is stable unless the protocol defines synchronization.

15
00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:06.048
Shutdown and failure are part of ownership.

16
00:01:06.048 --> 00:01:11.919
A peer may exit while the other side still has a mapping, descriptor, or outstanding operation.

17
00:01:11.919 --> 00:01:18.891
The design should prevent stale access from becoming normal behavior, release resources deterministically, and define whether the remaining process

18
00:01:18.891 --> 00:01:21.092
may continue using a transferred capability.

19
00:01:21.092 --> 00:01:25.129
The secure habit is to document resources as carefully as messages.

20
00:01:25.129 --> 00:01:34.669
Who can read, who can write, who can close, what size is valid, what synchronization is required, and what happens when either side fails should all

21
00:01:34.669 --> 00:01:35.403
be explicit.

22
00:01:35.403 --> 00:01:43.108
Reviewers should also look at how resources cross privilege boundaries, how duplicate handles are tracked, and whether cleanup still happens when

23
00:01:43.108 --> 00:01:44.943
requests are cancelled or retried.

24
00:01:44.943 --> 00:01:50.080
Ownership tests should include normal completion, denial, timeout, duplicate transfer, and peer exit paths.
